{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2246","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2246","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2246\",\"parent_revision\":\"f86c374f4afbac8f616484ff5642e861aba24aca\",\"root_revision\":\"cf1e2d1f6f19f5253178ec6cd551895f7d9a2b0fc8bb9c21eb1d4b6a0b16a4f3\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **add probe-phase auto-calibration to linear-walkthrough based on repository size** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/skill-generation-tools/codebase-recon-skill.md \u2014 pattern: Auto-Scaling Analysis (probe phase + size-based parameter calibration)\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-10\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-14)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n**Status: APPROVED-FOR-PLANNING**\\n\\n| Condition | Status | Source |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Functional intent | AVAILABLE | Item description + insights file |\\n| Scope boundaries | AVAILABLE | In-scope: SKILL.md Mermaid workflow + agent-instructions.md. Out-of-scope: #2249 git-history-recon, Improvement 3 (recon-report coupling) |\\n| External interfaces | AVAILABLE | Three probe commands fully specified; output files named |\\n| Constraints | DERIVABLE | Must not break existing medium-repo behavior; target dir may lack `.git/` (unaddressed edge case \u2014 see Questions for Human) |\\n| Existing system context | AVAILABLE | SKILL.md and agent-instructions.md fully read and verified |\\n| Verification expectations | AVAILABLE | Measurable signal explicitly stated in item |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 The current fixed-50k-token budget causes unnecessary agent overhead on small repos (spinning N tracing agents + M validators for a 5-file project) and under-coverage on large repos. Auto-calibration is a correctness improvement with a clear, low-risk implementation path. Source pattern from codebase-recon-skill is well-validated.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Bottleneck: small repos pay full multi-agent orchestration cost with no proportional benefit; large repos get insufficient per-agent read depth\\n- Blocks: no downstream items blocked by absence of this feature\\n- Enables: #2249 (git-history-recon) and Improvement 3 (recon-report coupling) both assume probe infrastructure will exist in linear-walkthrough; this item establishes the Phase 0 hook they would integrate into\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Small repo users get a faster, cheaper walkthrough (single tracing agent, no validation rotation)\\n- Large repo users get tighter per-agent budgets and enforced validation rotation, reducing missed coverage\\n- The `size_class:` classification in `repo-vitals.md` becomes a machine-readable signal that future integrations (e.g., #2249's recon report) can consume\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen `/linear-walkthrough` is invoked, before Phase 1 Discovery begins, the orchestrator runs three probe commands against the target directory, writes results to `walkthrough/repo-vitals.md`, and selects a size class. The Discovery agent reads the calibration block from `coverage-plan.md` and adjusts: small repos produce exactly 1 tracing agent with no cross-validation rotation; medium repos retain current behavior; large repos use a tighter 25k-token per-agent budget with larger N and mandatory validation rotation.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nThe target observable state after implementation:\\n\\n- The linear-walkthrough Mermaid diagram in `SKILL.md` has a Phase 0 node (\\\"Repo Probe\\\") inserted before Phase 1 Discovery, with a branch handling the non-git-repo edge case\\n- `walkthrough/repo-vitals.md` is produced on every run containing commit count, source file count, contributor count, and a `size_class: small|medium|large` line\\n- `coverage-plan.md` contains a `## Calibration` section specifying which size class was selected and how many tracing agents will spawn\\n- `references/agent-instructions.md` Discovery Agent Instructions include a step to read the calibration block and apply per-class agent count and token budget rules\\n- On a synthetic repo with fewer than 10 commits and fewer than 5 files, the orchestrator spawns exactly 1 tracing agent\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Running `/linear-walkthrough <target-dir>` on any git repo produces `walkthrough/repo-vitals.md` containing all three probe values and the line `size_class: small|medium|large` \u2014 verifiable with `grep \\\"size_class:\\\" walkthrough/repo-vitals.md`\\n2. `walkthrough/coverage-plan.md` contains a `## Calibration` section \u2014 verifiable with `grep \\\"## Calibration\\\" walkthrough/coverage-plan.md`\\n3. On a synthetic repo with fewer than 500 commits and fewer than 100 source files: the orchestrator spawns exactly 1 tracing agent (observable from orchestrator log / agent spawn count)\\n4. On a medium repo (500\u201310k commits, 100\u20131000 files): the orchestrator behaves identically to the current implementation (N agents, M validators, 50k token budget per agent)\\n5. On a large repo (>10k commits, >1000 files): each tracing agent receives a 25k token budget constraint and validation rotation is enforced\\n6. The existing `linear-walkthrough` workflow continues to work end-to-end on the `claude_skills` repo (regression check)\\n7. The Mermaid workflow diagram in `SKILL.md` reflects the Phase 0 probe step \u2014 verifiable by reading the diagram after the change\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n- What should the orchestrator do when the target directory is not a git repo (no `.git/` directory)? Options: (a) skip probe, default to medium-class behavior; (b) use only file-count signal (omitting commit count and contributor count); (c) hard-fail with a clear message. The item's measurable signal only tests git repos \u2014 the non-git case is unaddressed.\\n- The item specifies source file count via `find` with explicit extensions (`.py`, `.ts`, etc.). Should the extension list be hardcoded in SKILL.md, or should the probe auto-detect the dominant languages first and then count? The item's insights file uses a placeholder (`...`) for the extension list.\\n- The thresholds (small: <500 commits OR <100 source files; medium: 500-10k / 100-1000; large: >10k / >1000) use OR logic for small and implicit AND logic for large \u2014 should the classification use OR or AND when commit count and file count signals disagree (e.g., 200 commits but 5000 files)?\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work (skill to modify) | `/home/user/claude_skills/.claude/skills/linear-walkthrough/SKILL.md` \u2014 Mermaid workflow diagram (lines 24-101) |\\n| Prior work (agent instructions) | `/home/user/claude_skills/.claude/skills/linear-walkthrough/references/agent-instructions.md` \u2014 Discovery Agent Instructions, lines 1-51; Tracing Agent constraints line 61 |\\n| Prior work (output format) | `/home/user/claude_skills/.claude/skills/linear-walkthrough/references/output-format.md` |\\n| Research | `/home/user/claude_skills/research/insights/2026-05-10-codebase-recon-skill-improvements.md` \u2014 Improvement 1 (full probe design with target state, measurable signal) |\\n| Source pattern | `/home/user/claude_skills/research/skill-generation-tools/codebase-recon-skill.md` \u2014 Auto-Scaling Analysis section, lines 37-47 |\\n| Related backlog item | #2249 \u2014 git-history-recon skill (depends on probe pattern established here) |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 this is a standalone skill modification\\n- Blocks: #2249 (git-history-recon) would benefit from the probe infrastructure; Improvement 3 (Discovery agent consuming recon report) depends on #2249 which depends on this\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 The change is confined to two files: `SKILL.md` (update the Mermaid diagram and add Phase 0 node text) and `references/agent-instructions.md` (add calibration-reading step to Discovery Agent Instructions). The probe commands are standard git and POSIX shell. No new agents, no new files beyond the output artifacts. The source pattern is fully specified in the research insights file with exact commands and thresholds.\\n\\n## Source Pattern\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nQuote from research entry section \\\"Key Features\\\" (lines 37-47): \\\"Auto-Scaling Analysis \u2014 The skill runs a Probe phase to determine repository size and calibrates analysis parameters: Small repos (<500 commits): analyze entire history, return 10 results. Medium repos (500-10k): analyze last 1 year, return 20 results. Large repos (>10k): analyze last 6 months, return 30 results.\\\"\\n\\nExternal tool: codebase-recon-skill (https://github.com/yujiachen-y/codebase-recon-skill)\\nInsights file: ./research/insights/2026-05-10-codebase-recon-skill-improvements.md (Improvement 1)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA Phase 0 \\\"Repo Probe\\\" step is added at the top of the linear-walkthrough Mermaid workflow, executed by the orchestrator before Phase 1 Discovery. The probe runs three commands and writes their results to `walkthrough/repo-vitals.md`:\\n\\n1. `git rev-list --count HEAD` \u2014 total commits\\n2. Source file count via `find` or equivalent\\n3. `git shortlog -sn --no-merges | wc -l` \u2014 total contributors\\n\\nThe probe result classifies the repo as small (<500 commits OR <100 source files), medium (500-10k commits, 100-1000 files), or large (>10k commits, >1000 files), and writes a calibration block at the top of `coverage-plan.md`. The Discovery agent reads this calibration block and adjusts:\\n\\n- Small: single Tracing agent, no cross-validation rotation, skip synthesis-validation if N=1\\n- Medium: current behavior (N agents, M validators)\\n- Large: tighter per-agent token budget (25k instead of 50k), larger N, validation rotation enforced\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAfter the change, running `/linear-walkthrough` on a target directory produces `walkthrough/repo-vitals.md` containing the three probe values and a classification line `size_class: small|medium|large`. The string `size_class:` is grep-able. `coverage-plan.md` contains a `## Calibration` section that names which class was selected and how many tracing agents will spawn. On a small synthetic repo (<10 commits, <5 files), exactly 1 tracing agent is spawned; on a medium repo, the default N>=2 spawns; the orchestrator log records the probe was consulted before Phase 1.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`./.claude/skills/linear-walkthrough/SKILL.md` uses a fixed 50k-token-per-agent read budget regardless of repository size (lines 36, 53, 68, line 40 of references/agent-instructions.md). The Discovery phase (Phase 1) writes a coverage plan, but coverage plan agent assignments are sized only by total file bytes per file (`~4 characters per token` proxy at line 40 of agent-instructions.md). There is no orchestrator-level probe step that inspects repository size signals (commit count, file count, contributor count, age) before spawning the Discovery agent. The number of parallel Tracing agents (N) is determined by the Discovery agent splitting files into 50k-token assignments \u2014 small repos still pay the cost of full Discovery + multiple Tracing agents + Validation rotation; large repos receive the same fixed per-agent budget regardless of how much the codebase actually contains.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"e1cc7248d74b96a0b31ecfe3e174d5450723bf0f30475af0bde97c36824110cc\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPukGqA\"}"}